  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "What property does conditional probability have for two independent events?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(P(B\\mid A)\\) must equal \\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(P(B\\mid A)\\) must equal \\(1\\)",
    "C": "The probability that \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) occur simultaneously must equal \\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(P(B\\mid A)\\) equals \\(P(B)\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Assume \\(P(A)>0\\), so \\(P(B\\mid A)\\) is defined. Independence means that the occurrence of \\(A\\) does not change the probability of \\(B\\), because\n\\[\nP(B\\mid A)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}=\\frac{P(A)P(B)}{P(A)}=P(B).\n\\]\nThus choice D is correct whenever this conditional probability is defined.\n\nIf \\(P(A)=0\\), the elementary conditional-probability ratio is undefined. For example, \\(A=\\varnothing\\) is independent of every event \\(B\\), since \\(P(A\\cap B)=0=P(A)P(B)\\), but \\(P(B\\mid A)\\) is undefined. Therefore D requires the unstated condition \\(P(A)>0\\). This example also shows that mutually exclusive events can be independent when one has probability \\(0\\).",
